<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout xml:id="notes-week-01">
  <title>Week 1</title>

 <activity>
  

  <title>Exercise 1.2</title>
  
  
  <p>
    Explain the error in the following <q>proof</q> that <m>2=1</m>.
  </p>


 </activity>

   <proof>
    <p>
      Let <m>x=y</m>. Then,

      <md>
       <mrow> x^2\amp = xy </mrow>
       <mrow>x^2-y^2 \amp = xy-y^2 </mrow>
       <mrow>(x+y)(x-y) \amp = y(x-y)</mrow>
       <mrow>x+y \amp = y</mrow>
       <mrow>2y \amp = y</mrow>
       <mrow>2 \amp = 1</mrow>
      </md>
      
      
    </p>
  </proof>


  <activity>
    

    <title>Exercise 1.2</title>
    
    
    <p>
      Suppose that m and n are positive, odd integers.
Does there exists a perfect cover of the mn chessboard?
If I remove 1 square from the mn chessboard, will it have a perfect cover?

    </p>
  </activity>
</handout> 